=== minimal reproducer: exception handling in acb.integral ===
python-flint 0.9.0   FLINT via python-flint bindings
python 3.14.7

-- A. callback raises a Python exception
   outcome: raised:ValueError
   value/message: deliberate failure inside the callback
   callback invoked 11 times, raised 1

-- B. callback performs an invalid operation (1/sqrt at a ball containing 0)
   outcome: returned
   value/message: nan + nanj
   callback invoked 239999 times
   true value of int_0^1 sin(z)/sqrt(z) dz is about 0.6205366

-- C. callback returns a NON-FINITE value (documented protocol)
   outcome: returned
   value: nan
   finite? False   (a non-finite result is the SAFE signal)

-- D. the hardening actually used here
   outcome: REJECTED by the recorded flag
   recorded failure flag: True

-- LAYER ATTRIBUTION
   FLINT core knows nothing about Python exceptions; it calls a C
   function pointer and continues on its return value.  The adapter
   that wraps the Python callable for that pointer is in the
   python-flint BINDING.  When the Python callback raises, the adapter
   returns to C with a CPython exception still set; FLINT then keeps
   integrating with whatever was in the output variable.  The symptom
   'returned a result with an exception set' is CPython noticing this
   only once control returns to the interpreter.
   => BINDING-level defect (callback adapter), not FLINT core, and not
      this repository's wrapper -- though the repository's wrapper is
      what must defend against it, which is case D.
